INT. ROLLER RINK - FAMILY REUNION - DAY

A wood-floored rink under a mirror ball. Banner reads "WASHINGTON FAMILY 40TH." Folding tables of casserole line one wall. Disco hits thump faintly.

PEARL WASHINGTON, eighties, in a purple tracksuit, sits on a throne of folding chairs. UNCLE ROY, sixties, her son, rolls by on rented skates, arms out. TAMIKA, thirties, Roy's niece, balances a paper plate in one hand and her phone in the other. JUNIOR, fifteen, hangs on the rail, hoodie up. LILIANA, six, Tamika's daughter, sits cross-legged on the floor lacing her skates wrong.

PEARL
Roy! Skate like you got sense!

UNCLE ROY
I'm skating, Ma!

He is not. He wobbles. He grabs the rail beside Junior and holds on, grinning.

TAMIKA
Junior, take your hood off.

JUNIOR
It's fine.

TAMIKA
It's your grandmother's birthday.

Junior pushes the hood back an inch. Tamika sets her plate in front of Pearl, who pushes it back toward Tamika.

PEARL
That's not my food.

TAMIKA
It's your casserole.

PEARL
Then why is it cold?

Liliana tugs Tamika's sleeve.

LILIANA
Mama, my skates are on backwards.

TAMIKA
They're on your wrong feet, baby.

She kneels. Roy rolls up and lowers himself beside Pearl, and Pearl, without a word, pats his knee twice. Junior watches this from the rail and lets the hood fall all the way down.
